WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.181
Agents should receive only the permissions needed for approved business tasks.

2
00:00:04.181 --> 00:00:10.946
Least privilege limits the impact of misuse, compromise, configuration errors, and unintended behavior.

3
00:00:10.946 --> 00:00:19.377
The model may recommend an action, but trusted application code and identity systems should determine whether the agent is authorized to perform it.

4
00:00:19.377 --> 00:00:23.667
Natural-language output should never grant additional authority by itself.

5
00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:30.302
Permissions can be scoped by user, resource, action, environment, time, and workflow context.

6
00:00:30.302 --> 00:00:35.681
An agent that summarizes records may need read access but not permission to edit them.

7
00:00:35.681 --> 00:00:39.313
An agent that drafts a message may not need authority to send it.

8
00:00:39.313 --> 00:00:48.991
Separating read, draft, recommend, approve, and execute capabilities creates useful control points and makes business intent easier to review.

9
00:00:48.991 --> 00:00:54.499
Avoid broad standing credentials when task-specific or temporary access is practical.

10
00:00:54.499 --> 00:01:02.471
Protect tokens and secrets outside prompts and memory, limit their lifetime, monitor their use, and make revocation straightforward.

11
00:01:02.471 --> 00:01:11.660
Tool services should validate arguments and repeat authorization checks instead of trusting that a request is safe because it came from the agent application.

12
00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.504
Permission reviews should continue after deployment.

13
00:01:14.504 --> 00:01:20.351
New tools, data sources, workflow changes, and role changes can quietly expand access.

14
00:01:20.351 --> 00:01:28.822
Teams should compare actual use with approved need, remove unused capabilities, and test denied actions as carefully as allowed ones.

15
00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:36.854
A useful agent can still be tightly bounded when the system exposes focused tools rather than unrestricted administrative interfaces.
